@startuml
' Figure 5.3 – System sequence diagram: from reading events to dashboard metrics
!include ../_style.puml
!include ../_sequence.puml
mainframe interaction SequenceDiagram Analytics aggregation
autonumber "0 :"

participant "Reader" as U
participant "Browser\napplication" as W
participant "System" as A
participant "Worker" as K
participant "Database" as D

U -> W : Open an article
activate W
W -> W : Observe scroll depth and engaged time
W -> A : POST /analytics/events, batched\n(10 events, or every 5 s, or on unload)
activate A
A -> A : Take the viewer from the access token, when one is sent,\nand attach the country and device server-side
A -> D : Append to the raw event log
activate D
D --> A : Stored
deactivate D
deactivate A
deactivate W

|||

group Scheduled, every 5 minutes
  K -> D : Read events since the last aggregation
  activate K
  activate D
  D --> K : New events only
  K -> K : Compute views, unique views, read time,\ncompletion and engagement
  K -> D : Upsert the per-article metrics
  deactivate D
  deactivate K
end

group Scheduled, every 15 minutes, just after an article rollup
  K -> D : Read the per-article metrics and the social tables
  activate K
  activate D
  D --> K : Rows
  K -> K : Compute the audience, content and quality rollups
  K -> D : Upsert the 3 per-writer metric rows
  deactivate D
  deactivate K
end

' Eligibility is a separate marketplace job (marketplace.processor.ts), reading
' the raw events and the social tables rather than the rollups.
group Scheduled, every 20 minutes
  K -> D : Count identified readers and reactions\nof the writers not yet eligible
  activate K
  activate D
  D --> K : Counts
  K -> D : Mark the writers above both thresholds eligible
  deactivate D
  deactivate K
end

U -> W : Open the dashboard
activate W
W -> A : GET the analytics for an article
activate A
A -> D : Read the pre-computed rollup, one row
activate D
D --> A : Metrics
deactivate D
A --> W : Metrics
deactivate A
W --> U : Render the charts
deactivate W

note over U, D
  The dashboards never read the raw event log. Aggregation is
  incremental and idempotent, so a re-run cannot double-count.
end note
@enduml
